1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Okay, so welcome back to the computer
screen. What we're going to have a look at
now is a sensible and typical trading plan
for a beginner taking this professional
trading approach.

2
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:29,000
So we're going to use the example here
that we've already skimmed over in the
portfolio management section, the
portfolio parameter construction measures.

3
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:39,000
And we're going to set up a theoretical
margin account here with $25,000 deposited
on margin.

4
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,000
And as ever, we do want to give ourselves
a chance of making money.

5
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:50,000
So we're going to be setting gross
exposure limits so we don't actually ever
receive a margin call from our broker.

6
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:58,000
And we're looking here at a typical
situation in Europe where we're trading
leveraged CFDs.

7
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:06,000
And in the equities space, we're looking
at five times exposure rate, assuming a
well diversified longshore portfolio.

8
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:18,000
And also with currencies, an exposure rate
of between six to eight times and
commodities four to five times.

9
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:19,000
In the United States, things may be a
little bit different.

10
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
In the United States, things may be a
little bit different.

11
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,000
So just make sure with your broker what
your exposure rates can and can't be.

12
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:38,000
But typically, professional traders may
take an approach of five times equity
exposure, six to eight times currencies
and four to five times on commodities.

13
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:45,000
So really, to get up to that point, you've
got to be making some money first.

14
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:59,000
So we're applying this principle that
professional traders always apply, that
when we're losing money, we reduce
exposure because we don't have the right
to continue running the same amount of
capital as before.

15
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:09,000
But as we're winning, as we're making
money, we seek to increase our exposure
rates as we've got the right to commit
more capital.

16
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:19,000
So we're just going to go through this
example here where we've got $25,000
deposited on margin.

17
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Really, the minimum amount of capital you
can use to take this approach is around
15,000 US dollars.

18
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Below these levels, the amount of exposure
and the diversity that you need to take is
just not really possible.

19
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:47,000
Your risk will become too concentrated and
your position sizes, if you wanted to be
diverse, would just be too small.

20
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:54,000
So we're looking at $25,000 here and the
minimum is really $15,000.

21
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:06,000
You know, we don't pretend here that you
can open a trading account with $1,000 and
go 10 times leveraged in equities and 100
times leveraged in Forex.

22
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:13,000
We really just don't advocate that because
that's the typical retail to trader
strategy.

23
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Unfortunately, there is, if you want to
take the professional approach, a kind of
minimum barrier to entry in terms of
capital required.

24
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:28,000
So the minimum is $15,000 and here we're
going to use the example of $25,000.

25
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:34,000
And we're going to assume as well a 5%
return before we increase our exposure
rate.

26
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:44,000
So this may be over a couple of months
because we're using the one to three
months investment horizon, trading horizon
on all our positions.

27
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:58,000
So in step one, we're looking at $100,000
exposure across eight to 10 positions,
which means we're four times exposed on
our $25,000 that's been deposited.

28
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:08,000
If we're four times exposed, that means
we've got a 25% cushion, which means we
can lose 25% before we receive a margin
call.

29
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:18,000
However, if we're running a long, short,
diverse portfolio of eight to 10
positions, this is very, very hard to
achieve.

30
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:28,000
So this gives us a lot of comfort in terms
of not being stopped out or receiving a
margin call when we take our first step in
trading.

31
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Now, if we manage to make a 5% return on
our $100,000 over the first few months,
that means our trading account will have
exposure of 105 because our margin has
increased now from 25 to 30,000.

32
00:04:48,000 --> 00:05:00,000
And then we're increasing our exposure
rate up to five times because now we've
got the right to commit more capital, as
in take more exposure.

33
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:11,000
However, at the same time, we are actually
increasing our positioning, our positions
to 10 to 12 positions in the portfolio.

34
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Meaning that even though we're increasing
our exposure, we are actually increasing
our diversity.

35
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:25,000
So our risk may not actually be going up,
even though we're making money and our
exposure is higher.

36
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:34,000
The five times exposure rate gives us a
20% cushion rather than a 25% cushion on
four times exposure.

37
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
However, we are more diverse.

38
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:50,000
So as our exposure rates go up, what
you're going to find is that your P&L
swings will go up slightly, but you will
actually be more diverse.

39
00:05:50,000 --> 00:06:03,000
Then in step two, when we've got our
150,000 exposure and our margin of 30K, if
we assume a 5% return after another few
months.

40
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:08,000
So now let's assume that we've been
trading a total of say four to six months.

41
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:20,000
This is a sensible target in terms of
making another 5% and then your margin
increases to 37,500 US dollars.

42
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:36,000
And at this stage, we can really look to
start taking some risk here, where we
increase our exposure to six times what we
have deposited on margin, which means we
have $225,000 exposure.

43
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:45,000
And at the same time, we again increase
the amount of positions in our portfolio
to increase diversity.

44
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:56,000
And remember that the entire time that
this is happening, we are sticking to our
principles of risk management and our
principles of trading psychology.

45
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:11,000
So as we're increasing our exposure in
terms of psychology, we're attempting to
cut our losers and increase our winning
trades and replace them with fresh ideas
and also overlaying our portfolio.

46
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:23,000
So our 8 to 10 positions, our 10 to 12 and
our 12 to 14 positions overlaying each of
these portfolios with sensible risk
management parameters.

47
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:37,000
So if we look at the typical example here,
when we start out, a typical example here
may be the $100,000 portfolio, which we've
explained here, across 10 positions in
equities.

48
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:41,000
Now, at the moment, there's no hard and
fast rule here.

49
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,000
You can have some commodities exposure.

50
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,000
You can have some currencies exposure.

51
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:53,000
But to get used to this idea of building
portfolios around spread trades and
themes, this is a sensible approach to
take to begin with.

52
00:07:53,000 --> 00:08:00,000
So you're looking at an exposure of 50,000
on the long side, 50,000 on the short
side.

53
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,000
You can be beta hedged if you want.

54
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:04,000
That's totally fine.

55
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:09,000
But a gross exposure limit of 100,000
across 10 positions.

56
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:19,000
So five longs and five shorts with a
restriction on your net exposure, your net
positioning to market risk.

57
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:33,000
And what you can do here is actually when
you start out, because when you start out,
you will just have zero exposure in your
portfolio, is build the portfolio in two
stages.

58
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:48,000
So build each position in two stages by
taking a $5,000 position now and then a
$5,000 position later, given the
opportunities to get a better price or
prices on a breakout of the spreads.

59
00:08:48,000 --> 00:09:02,000
In terms of overlaying your portfolios
with sensible risk management parameters,
we've seen a lot of them already, but
let's run through some of the basics here
again.

60
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:08,000
What you may want to do here, and by the
way, this spreadsheet is available in the
downloads area.

61
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:15,000
What you may want to do here is have a
restriction on your net positioning.

62
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:23,000
So here in this mini portfolio, the
restriction is 35% net long or short
exposure.

63
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:33,000
I would say a sensible approach when
you're starting out is to just have a
maximum of net long or short exposure, a
limit of 50%.

64
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:37,000
Moving on to stop losses and targets.

65
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,000
Remember, every stop loss is a hard stop
loss.

66
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:46,000
You can't automatically set them in your
brokerage account because we're doing our
stop losses on spreads.

67
00:09:46,000 --> 00:10:01,000
And our hard stop losses on large cap and
mid cap spreads are somewhere between 7%
to 12%, meaning that our soft targets are
21% to 36%.

68
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:08,000
So you should apply this 1 to 3 rule on
your spread trades across your portfolio.

69
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:15,000
Also, you should apply and overlay really
a portfolio stop loss of about 10% on your
exposure.

70
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:26,000
So in our example that we've just been
through, $10,000 on your exposure in step
1 of $100,000 is really your stop loss on
your whole portfolio.

71
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:33,000
So if you lose $10,000 of your original
$25,000, then you've got $15,000 left.

72
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:37,000
You can live to fight another day and you
just start again.

73
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:47,000
You take a break, take a break from
trading for a couple of months and then
seek to build your watch list again, build
out your ideas and then begin again.

74
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
You never ever want to receive a margin
call.

75
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:56,000
So make sure you overlay your portfolio
with a stop loss on the entire portfolio.

76
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:07,000
And what will happen is because you've got
8 to 10 positions and you're putting a 7%
to 12% stop loss on all of your positions,

77
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:14,000
for this to actually occur, it means that
you'd have to get all of your spread
trades wrong, which is pretty unlikely.

78
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:23,000
So if all of your spread trades hit, say,
your 10% stop losses between 7% and 12%,
then you're just going to be stopped at.

79
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:30,000
And if you get every single spread trade
wrong, when you've got 8 to 10 positions,
so 4 to 5 spread trades,

80
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:34,000
if you get every single spread trade
wrong, then there's clearly something
drastically wrong.

81
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:37,000
So you should stop yourself out of your
whole portfolio.

82
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:40,000
You can, of course, beat a hedge where you
want to.

83
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:45,000
So if you want to take a little bit of
market risk, that's okay.

84
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:50,000
But just go through the beta exercises and
make sure that you've got the risk that
you want on

85
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:54,000
and the risk that you want to hedge out
hedged out.

86
00:11:54,000 --> 00:12:05,000
You should probably be seeking to get a
mix of cross-sector, cross-sector
constituent and intra-sector constituent
spread trades.

87
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:06,000
Just try them out.

88
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:09,000
It's all about experimentation in the
beginning.

89
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:12,000
So you don't have to go huge in all of
your trades.

90
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:20,000
Like I said, you can have starter
positions and seek to build out the trades
as you get better prices or on breakouts.

91
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:23,000
Because on breakouts, they'll be winning
trades already.

92
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:26,000
So you can seek to add to any of these
trades.

93
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:33,000
If you're going to do cross-currency
spread trades, it's probably not the best
thing to do to begin with.

94
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:42,000
But as you're growing and you do
cross-currency spread trades, if you don't
want the currency risk, make sure you
begin by hedging out the currency risk.

95
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:51,000
So you do the three trades that we talked
about in the example for hedging out
cross-currency spread trade currency risk.

96
00:12:51,000 --> 00:13:03,000
And as you're making money, as you're
going from step one to step two and to
step three over the first six months, when
you're making money, you can seek to
increase your exposure rates.

97
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:08,000
Make sure you're increasing your diversity
and not concentrating your risk.

98
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:15,000
And also, you may seek to take positions
in currencies that you actually have a
view on.

99
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:20,000
So this is very different to hedging out
the currency risk on a spread trade.

100
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:24,000
Because if you have no view on the
currency, you should just hedge it out.

101
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:31,000
But if you do have a view on the currency,
you can take, as you're growing, positions
in currencies.

102
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:34,000
And remember, they're going to be less
volatile than equities.

103
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:37,000
And you can take positions in commodities.

104
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:45,000
This will help you as a trader develop
over the medium to longer term, where you
can have exposure in different asset
classes.

105
00:13:45,000 --> 00:14:00,000
And when you're taking currency exposure,
as a rule of thumb, try to restrict your
currency positioning exposure to the size
of your equities exposure in your trading
account.

106
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:04,000
But still take a one to three month view.

107
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:05,000
So what do we mean by that?

108
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
Well, let's say you're in step two.

109
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:15,000
And you've got $150,000 in exposure in
your portfolio in terms of equities
exposure.

110
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:21,000
Then if you're going to take currency
positions, try to restrict them to
$150,000.

111
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:30,000
So don't go over the amount of your
equities exposure when you're taking
currencies exposure.

112
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:41,000
And currencies, we've seen already, are a
lot less volatile than equities as a rule,
as a general rule across the board, across
the major currency pairs.

113
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:47,000
So that type of exposure is about right
for beginners.

114
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:55,000
So what you will see if you've got a
margin trading account and your broker,
for example, allows you to take a hundred
times exposure.

115
00:14:55,000 --> 00:15:03,000
Well, what you will see is that you're
actually just utilizing a very small
amount of your margin when you restrict
yourself to that size.

116
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:13,000
And then if and when these currency
positions actually make money and become
winning trades, then they become an option
to seek more risk.

117
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:22,000
So start off with no more than the
equities exposure, the corresponding
equities exposure in your portfolio.

118
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:28,000
No more exposure than that in a currency
position as a starter position.

119
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:39,000
Remember the whole time we want to be
pulling from box A when getting paid and
pulling from box B when paying out.

120
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:48,000
But we need to make sure that we're
cutting our losing trades, making sure
that we don't take more exposure there.

121
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:57,000
And when we're getting paid, make sure
that we're pulling from box A and looking
to run our winning positions, not take
profits.

122
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:01,000
Run your winning positions and actually
seek more risk.

123
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:13,000
And in terms of recycling, well, you're
always going to have a portfolio and
you're going to be running your winners
and cutting your losers.

124
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:17,000
Your ideas from your watch list have to
replace your losers.

125
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:22,000
So make sure that you're always
maintaining this watch list every week.

126
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:28,000
Every week you should probably look at
between 8 to 12 hours per week.

127
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:31,000
This can be done with a full time job.

128
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
8 to 12 hours per week.

129
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:36,000
Maybe 3 to 4 hours on a Wednesday evening.

130
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:39,000
3 to 4 hours on a Saturday or Sunday
morning.

131
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
So you're prepared for the week.

132
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
Updating all of your spreads.

133
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:46,000
Generating new, fresh trading ideas.

134
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:52,000
And a couple of hours during the week
you'll be going into your trading account
as well.

135
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
You know, just to check up on things.

136
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
You don't need to be trading every day.

137
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:59,000
Your time horizon here is 1 to 3 months.

138
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:02,000
And everything should be reviewed on a
week to week basis.

139
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:07,000
So a couple of hours during the week
you'll be going into your trading account.

140
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:10,000
In total a few hours to check things
through.

141
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
Maybe just 10 minutes every day.

142
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:16,000
And on a Wednesday evening budget 3 to 4
hours.

143
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
So you can update your watch list.

144
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
Check on the spreads.

145
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:24,000
Make sure your losers aren't losing too
much money.

146
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
Or hitting your stop losses.

147
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
So you can trade out to them if you need
to.

148
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:29,000
Running your winners.

149
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:30,000
Possibly adding to them.

150
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:33,000
And then doing the same on a Saturday or
Sunday.

151
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
Planning for the Monday.

152
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:45,000
Make sure your ideas are fresh and
current.

153
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:49,000
If something's been on your watch list for
a very, very long time.

154
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:56,000
Then you should probably be seeking to
actually take it off the watch list and
replace it with new ideas.

155
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
A long time maybe 2 or 3 months.

156
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
It doesn't mean it's a bad idea.

157
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
It just means it could be a stale idea.

158
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
Or you're just far too early.

159
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
It's going to have to be your call.

160
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
There's no rules here.

161
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:15,000
So just try and maintain a fresh and
current updated watch list.

162
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
And stick to the plan.

163
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
You know, stick to these.

164
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:20,000
Stick to this investment process.

165
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
Stick to the trading process.

166
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:25,000
Make sure your ideas are current.

167
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:28,000
Always maintain your world view.

168
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:31,000
Make sure you're always up to date with
the leading indicators.

169
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:36,000
With modern technology, you can set
yourself up to receive alerts on your
telephone.

170
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:39,000
You can get feeds into your email.

171
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
It's all very easy to keep on top of.

172
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:45,000
Once you've learned what the leading
indicators actually mean.

173
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:49,000
And how you can use them and interpret
them to make money.

174
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:52,000
To make real hard dollars in your
portfolio.

175
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:56,000
Make sure your watch list is always
updated in the gatekeeping process.

176
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:59,000
Stay away from bad ideas.

177
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:03,000
Keep good ideas on your watch list.

178
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,000
As in ideas that are making money.

179
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:07,000
And making money consistently.

180
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
But try to time them right.

181
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:12,000
Get the technical analysis and the price
action right.

182
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:17,000
Make sure when you're looking at your
opportunities.

183
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:21,000
That your risk management is outlined
correctly.

184
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:26,000
Always have a look at the distribution and
the ATR.

185
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
Check the historical volatility.

186
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:32,000
Make sure the opportunity is actually
there to make money.

187
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
And in risk management.

188
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
Make sure that your stop losses are
sensible.

189
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:40,000
And your targets are actually sensible and
